  "headline": "Oil shock meets a dollar squeeze: gold at its lows says stagflation, not fear",
  "body": [
    "The dashboard reads as a stagflationary squeeze, not a reflation. Brent is up 80% year to date and the 10-year yield sits just under 5%, so the inflation pressure is energy-led and policy is being held tight by it. Over the past month, though, yields have fallen about 20 basis points and copper has slipped 3%, which says growth is bending under that pressure rather than running with it.",
    "Gold is the tell. It has dropped 7% over the month to the bottom of its 52-week range while long yields fell, and gold falling as yields fall is not a safe-haven market; it is a dollar and liquidity story. The dollar index is up about 1% on the month and sits at 95% of its range, draining the bid from every dollar-priced asset. Bitcoin is losing less than gold, with ounces per bitcoin up 2.6% on the month, and the copper/gold ratio's 4% rise flatters growth only because gold fell faster than copper.",
    "The curve supports the same read. Over three months the 10-year rose 34 basis points against 18 for the 30-year, a bear-flattening that prices a central bank pinned by energy inflation rather than a fiscal term-premium scare. This week's 15 basis point rally in the 10-year with Brent flat is the first hint of a growth scare taking the lead.",
    "The war is the engine behind the oil leg. MintPress News reports Iran threatening a war without limits in retaliation for what it describes as US-Israeli operations, and Drop Site's War Brief pairs Iran's promised response with a Trump threat to the global economy. The channel is Gulf supply and shipping: escalation is bullish for Brent, supportive of the dollar, and a relative tailwind for the loonie, which has already gained with USD/CAD down 0.9% on the month. Yet Brent fell 1.6% today and realized volatility sits well below its own median, so markets have priced the conflict in the level, not the headlines, and are treating the rhetoric as rhetoric until supply is actually hit.",
    "That leaves one real tension: a regional war with gold at a 52-week low. The dollar and rates read is weighted here, because it explains gold, bitcoin and copper together, while a fear read explains none of them. If the threats become disruption, Brent and the 10-year would rise together and the squeeze would deepen; USD/INR, up nearly 6% this year, is where an oil escalation would bite hardest among the currencies on the page."
  ],
